    {
      "id": "18649",
      "key": "SFCC-323",
      "summary": "Evaluate Organisation-Level Content Model Templates for Schema Governance",
      "description": "As a\n Solution Architect,\nI want\n to understand whether Contentful's new Organisation-level Content Model Templates feature should replace or complement our script-based schema-as-code pipeline,\nso that\n we don't keep investing in CI automation for an approach Contentful itself may now handle natively across environments.\nContentful has moved content model templates into Organisation settings (no longer environment-level) — a platform change that postdates the original SFCC-123/SFCC-249 TSD design, which assumed schema was solely managed via \nscripts/contentful/content-types/*.js\n + the Management API. This story is a short technical evaluation: does the new org-level template feature offer a native way to define and propagate content types across our dev/test/sit/staging/uat/production environments, and if so, does it reduce, replace, or complement the current Git-managed script approach? Output is a short recommendation (keep as-is / adopt org templates / hybrid), not an implementation.\nScope: investigation and recommendation only. Any resulting schema-pipeline change is a follow-up story, not part of this one.",
      "status": {
        "name": "To Do",
        "category": "To Do",
        "color": "blue-gray"
      },
      "issue_type": {
        "name": "Story"
      },
      "created": "2026-08-04T11:57:07.986+1000",
      "updated": "2026-08-04T11:57:08.224+1000"
    },
    {
      "id": "18648",
      "key": "SFCC-322",
      "summary": "Contentful Schema-as-Code: Wire Sync/Migrate/Drift-Detection into CI",
      "description": "As a\n Developer,\nI want\n content-type schema changes to sync and validate automatically in CI when a PR touches \nscripts/contentful/content-types/\n,\nso that\n schema drift between environments is caught before merge instead of relying on someone remembering to run \nnpm run sync:contentful\n by hand.\nnpm run sync:contentful\n, \nmigrate:contentful\n, and \ndetect:drift\n all exist today but are run manually by a developer against their own local Management API token — there's no CI enforcement. This story wires those into the CI pipeline (using the CI service-account CMA token already stored as a GitHub Actions secret) so schema changes are validated and applied consistently, and drift is caught automatically rather than discovered later in a broken environment.\nScope: CI wiring only — assumes the existing sync/migrate/drift scripts as the mechanism (see the Content Model Templates story for whether that mechanism should change).",
      "status": {
        "name": "To Do",
        "category": "To Do",
        "color": "blue-gray"
      },
      "issue_type": {
        "name": "Story"
      },
      "created": "2026-08-04T11:57:03.706+1000",
      "updated": "2026-08-04T11:57:03.918+1000"
    },
    {
      "id": "18647",
      "key": "SFCC-321",
      "summary": "Contentful Publish Webhook: Cache-Purge Receiver & Per-Environment Wiring",
      "description": "As a\n Content editor,\nI want\n the storefront's SSR cache to clear automatically within seconds of me publishing a Contentful entry,\nso that\n I don't have to wait out the 60-second TTL or manually trigger a cache flush to see my change live.\nToday there is no publish-webhook receiver in the MRT Node layer — this is a documented target in \n02-contentful.md\n and the SFCC-249 TSD, not yet built. This story builds the HMAC-verified \nPOST /webhooks/contentful\n receiver (verifying \nX-Contentful-Signature\n against \nCMS_WEBHOOK_SECRET\n, rejecting invalid signatures with 401 and no processing), and configures the actual webhook in each Contentful environment to point at it.\nScope: the receiver implementation and its per-environment Contentful-side webhook configuration. Does not cover the SSR-first fetch migration itself (separate, larger effort referenced in the TSD Section 5.2).",
      "status": {
        "name": "To Do",
        "category": "To Do",
        "color": "blue-gray"
      },
      "issue_type": {
        "name": "Story"
      },
      "created": "2026-08-04T11:56:59.083+1000",
